  "notes": "The Texas producer-steel-player the research calls \"the analogue bench's centre of gravity\", and the entry's own summary of his board is the instruction this model follows: his records span the whole archetype — the acoustic-band shuffle on the Grammy-winning bluegrass-instrumented album, the hillbilly-boogie shuffle on Wayne Hancock's records (he has produced every Hancock album since the 1995 debut, which makes him the documented board behind the \"juke joint swing\" idiom), and a straight rock backbeat on the alt-country records — so \"model the drum preset as *idiom-selected*, with a small dry kit and minimal processing as the constant\". The constant is what is authored: velocity tiers 98–118 over 70–88 with ghosts at 26–42 (small and dry, well under the genre's 104–127 over 74–92), humanize at 0.72/0.22, a hat that stays out of the way at 0.26 and a ride/shaker pair at −7 dB. The boogie half of the catalogue is the kick: offbeat8thShare 0.14 with densityPerBar 2–4 is the doubled hillbilly-boogie kick sitting on top of the two-beat anchors. His own playing is the melody block — \"pedal steel as the lead colour … long sustained bends, pedal-and-lever chord changes under a held note, and volume-pedal swells\" — which is a low note density (2–5), legato articulation, a descend-weighted contour and the pedal_steel timbre hint; dobro and mandolin as second colours are the counter's three-style stack at a deliberately low ratio, because the entry states his records \"keep instrumental breaks proportionally long and dynamics narrow\". Keys carry the pedal-steel-friendly flats (F, B♭, E♭) alongside the guitar keys exactly as the entry specifies, \"where the steel leads\". Harmony is I–IV–V with V7, twelve-bar frames and the western-swing ii–V turnaround. ⚠ INFERRED: bpm.mode 120 (the entry gives a 55–190 band with no centre), swing.amount 0.65, all velocity tiers, ghost probability and positions, and the hat/percs densities. The research marks the groove 